🚀 Optimizing Spark Jobs: From 20,000 DPU Hours to 55 DPU Hours!
I'm excited to share a recent optimization we achieved on our production job running on AWS Glue, which originally consumed 20,281 DPU hours. By simply rewriting PySpark SQL syntax into PySpark DataFrame API, I was able to reduce the runtime to just 55 DPU hours!

Key Takeaways:
Switching to PySpark DataFrame API: 
The DataFrame API allows for more fine-grained control over optimizations like broadcast joins, partitioning, and caching, which improved memory usage and reduced the need for expensive shuffles.

Why AQE (Adaptive Query Execution) Didn't Fully Optimize PySpark SQL:
PySpark SQL adds an overhead because Spark has to parse and convert SQL queries into logical plans before executing them, which limits AQE’s effectiveness.
DataFrame API makes operations explicit, allowing AQE to dynamically optimize operations like shuffles, join strategies, and partitioning more effectively.
Using broadcast joins with the DataFrame API ensured that smaller datasets were joined efficiently, avoiding costly shuffles that PySpark SQL didn't handle as well.

Results:
Before Optimization: 20,281 DPU hours
After Optimization: 55 DPU hours
Cost Savings: 💸 Massive!

It’s incredible how a relatively small change—switching from SQL-based operations to the DataFrame API—made such a huge difference in job performance and cost savings. I encourage anyone facing performance issues with Spark to explore the DataFrame API for more efficient control over their Spark jobs.
